        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T02:19:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is there a better way to kick the state machines? Can we have a fake bit which would allow us to clear it and let the power task get kicked from the event. That way we aren\u0027t running things from different tasks -- let lone interrupt handlers.",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8750ec7f_57750323",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T07:29:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree it would be cleaner to kick the state machine without affecting any of its states directly. But the problem that I encountered was:\n1. This flag cannot be added as a power signal since all power signals are treated as gpios which are read to identify the value\n2. If we decide to simply add a global flag, I believe it would have to go in power/skylake.c (or to generalize in power/\u003cchipset\u003e.c) that will have to implement a function like chipset_handle_espi_reset(). For skylake, this will simply release the power button press and let the rest of state machine handle things on its own. But this chipset_handle_espi_reset will have to be provided by all chipsets that want to handle the espi_reset.\n\nDoes #2 above sound like a safer way?",
      "parentUuid": "921cc968_d1c30148",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    }
